The bug is that the goroutine closure captures the loop variable `i` by reference, not by value.

In Go 1.21, the loop variable `i` is a single variable that gets reused for each iteration. By the time the goroutines actually run, the loop has already finished and `i` is `5`, so every goroutine prints `5`.

The problematic lines are:

```go
go func() {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}()
```

Fix it by passing `i` into the goroutine as a function argument so each goroutine gets its own copy:

```go
go func(i int) {
    defer wg.Done()
    fmt.Println(i)
}(i)
```

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

