The bug is integer overflow in the multiplication:

`result *= i;`

A `u64` can only hold values up to:

`18446744073709551615`

The largest factorial that fits in a `u64` is:

`20! = 2432902008176640000`

The next one, however, does not:

`21! = 51090942171709440000`

So when `factorial(25)` reaches the iteration where `i == 21`, the multiplication overflows a `u64`.

In a Rust release build, unsigned integer overflow wraps silently modulo `2^64` when overflow checking is disabled. That means the high bits are discarded, and later multiplications continue from the truncated value. The final number is effectively `25! mod 2^64`, not the true value of `25!`.

One important caveat: with default Rust debug builds, this exact code would not silently “work” for `factorial(25)`; it would panic on the overflow at `i == 21`. If your debug run did not panic, overflow checking was probably disabled, or you only tested `factorial(20)`.

The root issue is that `25!` cannot be represented in a `u64` at all:

`25! = 15511210043330985984000000`

A `u128` is large enough for `25!`. Using checked multiplication also prevents the same kind of silent wrapping for inputs that are too large for `u128`.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn factorial(n: u64) -> u128 {
    let mut result: u128 = 1;
    for i in 1..=n {
        result = result
            .checked_mul(i as u128)
            .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!("factorial overflow for n = {}", n));
    }
    result
}

fn main() {
    println!("{}", factorial(20));
    println!("{}", factorial(25));
}
</solution>

